            <ArticleTitle>Macrotroponin causing elevation in cardiac troponin I.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>956.e5-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cjca.2014.03.037</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0828-282X(14)00223-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a case of macrotroponin causing persistently raised levels of cardiac troponin I (cTnI) in a 57-year-old man with recurrent atypical chest pain. Macrotroponin, a troponin-immunoglobulin complex, should be considered when cTnI values are inconsistent with the clinical picture, and fail to demonstrate a rise and/or fall pattern in suspected cases of acute coronary syndrome. Clear and effective communication between cardiologists and laboratorians is essential in the management of patients with macrotroponin.</AbstractText>
